.TH velsym "" "Command"
.SH NAME
velsym \- a drawing becomes a stencil
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvelsym\fR [\fB\-pfx\fR \fIp\fR] [\fB\-org\fR \fIx,y\fR] [\fB\-scale\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-type\fR \fINAME=t\fR] \fICODE file.d\fR >> \fIlib.sym\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
velsym -- a drawing becomes a stencil
Usage:	velsym [-pfx p] [-org x,y] [-scale n] [-type NAME=t]
		CODE file.d >> lib.sym
	-pfx p	designator prefix (R, C, U, ...)
	-org x,y	the grid point that becomes 0,0 (default: 1st pin)
	-scale n	the sketch is n times oversize
	-type NAME=t	pin NAME has type t (i o p b)
.HE
.B velsym
promotes an ordinary drawing into one
.B "symbol ... end"
block on standard output, so a shop's own standards get made ON THE
BOARD instead of hand-cut in
.B symedit
at quarter-grid coordinates.
.I CODE
is the name drawings will call the stencil by.
.PP
.B L
and
.B W
become
.B s
segments, a
.B B
box its four, a
.B P
polyline its n\-1,
.B C
a circle,
.B A
an arc angles and all, and a
.B T
whose text is ONE CHARACTER a
.B t
glyph.
Everything else \- shapes, connectors, dimensions, nested symbols and
multi-character text \- is SKIPPED AND COUNTED on standard error,
because a silently half-converted stencil is a part that draws wrong
forever.
.SH PINS
The pins come from the
.B N
net-name markers, in the order they appear in the file (which is the
z-order, and which
.B "velnet -spice"
reads when a library does not name its pins); the net name becomes the
pin name, and an
.B N
called
.B \-
is a pin with no name.
.PP
Pin TYPES come from
.BI \-type " NAME=t"
on the command line and NEVER from the drawing: a type is a property
of the library, and a guessed type builds a wrong ERC.
.I t
is
.B i
input,
.B o
output,
.B p
power or
.B b
bidirectional.
.SH COORDINATES
Coordinates are exact by construction.
A stencil is in quarter-grid units and a drawing in whole ones, so the
conversion is x4 and every pin lands on a multiple of 4 \- which is
what the loader wants.
.PP
.BI \-scale " n"
divides instead, for a stencil sketched n times oversize so its detail
was drawable at all; at
.B "-scale 4"
a drawing unit IS a quarter unit, and a hand-cut stencil's detail is
available.
.PP
.BI \-org " x,y"
names the grid point that becomes (0,0); the default is the FIRST PIN,
because a stencil's origin is where it snaps.
A sketch with no
.B N
markers at all falls back to the top-left of its printable extent, and
is told on standard error that it has no pins.
.SH "LIBRARIES AS BUILD PRODUCTS"
A stencil is a drawing, and a library is a build product:
.DS
.ta 8n
	STENCILS = pump.d valve.d tank.d
	pid.sym: $(STENCILS)
		rm -f $@
		velsym -pfx P  PUMP  pump.d  >> $@
		velsym -pfx V  VALVE valve.d >> $@
		velsym -pfx TK TANK  tank.d  >> $@
		velcheck -sym $@
	card: pid.sym ; velinfo -symsheet pid.sym | velplot -T ps -fit -
.DE
A stencil SKETCH is an ordinary drawing: it opens in the editor,
prints,
.BR velpic s
into the manual, and takes the whole revision workflow (see
.BR veldiff )
unchanged \- so the shop's stencils get revision control because they
ARE drawings.
Adding a stencil is adding a line; deleting one is deleting a file;
there is no library format to learn and no state anywhere but files.
.PP
/usr/vellum/etc/library.mk is the skeleton, and /usr/vellum/eg/sk holds
the nine sketches that build the stock
.B pid
library \- byte for byte, which is also this converter's fix point.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B symedit ,
.B velcheck ,
.B velinfo ,
.B make
.SH NOTES
The same conversion has an interactive face.
The editor's
.B "Make Symbol"
item (the
.B y
key) runs this program on the SELECTION, and
.B symedit
grows
.B "Import drawing"
(the
.B i
key), which brings a drawing into the edit buffer under exactly these
rules \- so the promote-then-touch-up workflow is one set of rules in
one place, and a stencil made from the board is byte-identical to one
made from a Makefile.
